      "source": [
        " 1. What is unsupervised learning in the context of machine learning?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer:  A type of machine learning where the algorithm learns from unlabeled data to find hidden patterns, structures, or groupings without human supervision.\n",
        "2. How does K-Means clustering algorithm work?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: It iteratively assigns data points to the nearest cluster centroid and then updates the centroids to be the mean of all points assigned to that cluster until convergence.\n",
        "3. Explain the concept of a dendrogram in hierarchical clustering.\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: A tree-like diagram that illustrates how clusters are merged (agglomerative) or split (divisive) at various distance thresholds, showing the hierarchical relationship between data points.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. What is the main difference between K-Means and Hierarchical Clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: K-Means requires you to pre-specify the number of clusters (K) and partitions data flatly, whereas Hierarchical Clustering builds a tree of clusters without needing Kin advance and can handle non-flat structures.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. What are the advantages of DBSCAN over K-Means?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: DBSCAN can discover clusters of arbitrary shapes, handles noise/outliers effectively, and does not require the user to specify the number of clusters beforehand.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. When would you use Silhouette Score in clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: To evaluate the quality of clustering by measuring how similar a data point is to its own cluster compared to other clusters, helping to determine the optimal number of clusters.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. What are the limitations of Hierarchical Clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: It has a high computational and memory complexity (O(N^3 ) or O(N^2  log⁡N )), making it inefficient for large datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "8. Why is feature scaling important in clustering algorithms like K-Means?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: Because K-Means relies heavily on Euclidean distance calculations; unscaled features with larger numeric ranges would disproportionately dominate the distance metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "9. How does DBSCAN identify noise points?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: Points that do not have the minimum required number of neighbor points (min_samples) within their specified neighborhood radius (eps) are classified as noise (outliers).\n",
        "\n",
        "10. Define inertia in the context of K-Means.\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: The sum of squared distances of samples to their closest cluster centroid, representing how internally coherent or compact the clusters are.\n",
        "\n",
        "11. What is the elbow method in K-Means clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: A heuristic method used to find the optimal number of clusters by plotting inertia against different values of Kand looking for the \"elbow\" point where the rate of decrease sharply shifts.\n",
        "\n",
        "12. Describe the concept of \"density\" in DBSCAN.\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: The number of data points located within a specific radius (eps) surrounding a given point.\n",
        "\n",
        "13. Can hierarchical clustering be used on categorical data?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: Yes, provided you use an appropriate distance or dissimilarity metric suitable for categorical variables (such as Gower's distance or Hamming distance) instead of standard Euclidean distance.\n",
        "\n",
        "14. What does a negative Silhouette Score indicate?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: It indicates that a sample has been assigned to the wrong cluster, as it is closer to the neighboring cluster than to the one it was assigned to.\n",
        "\n",
        "15. Explain the term \"linkage criteria\" in hierarchical clustering.\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: The rule or algorithm used to determine the distance between sets of observations based on the pairwise distances between their individual points (e.g., single, complete, average, or Ward linkage).\n",
        "\n",
        "16. Why might K-Means clustering perform poorly on data with varying cluster sizes or densities?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: Because K-Means uses Euclidean distance and assumes spherical, uniformly sized, and equally dense clusters, causing it to misclassify boundaries when densities or scales vary.\n",
        "\n",
        "17. What are the core parameters in DBSCAN, and how do they influence clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: eps (the maximum distance between two samples for one to be considered as in the neighborhood of the other) and min_samples (the number of samples in a neighborhood for a point to be considered a core point). Together, they control the density threshold required to form clusters.\n",
        "\n",
        "18. How does K-Means++ improve upon standard K-Means initialization?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: It selects initial centroids with a probability proportional to their squared distance from existing centroids, spreading them out and leading to faster convergence and more stable, better-quality clustering results.\n",
        "\n",
        "19. What is agglomerative clustering?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: A bottom-up approach to hierarchical clustering where each data point starts in its own cluster, and pairs of clusters are successively merged as one moves up the hierarchy.\n",
        "\n",
        "20. What makes Silhouette Score a better metric than just inertia for model evaluation?\n",
        "\n",
        "\t- Correct Answer: While inertia always decreases as Kincreases (favoring more clusters), the Silhouette Score evaluates both cohesion (how tight clusters are) and separation (how distinct clusters are from each other), providing a balanced measure that penalizes over-clustering.\n",
        "\n"
